=== layout: title-slide

# Lecture 07D

## Chi-Square Method (&chi;^2^)

## Chapter 7

## Dr. Neil Voss

## October 20, 2026

@notes

Source: lect07d-chi_square.odp, slide 1.

=== layout: one-panel

# *Chi-Square (&chi;^2^) Test*

@body

- Compares observed and expected data sets.
  - Expected data set was predicted by the null hypothesis
  - Measures the difference between observed and expected data.
- Evaluates the "_goodness of fit_" between observed and expected.
- If the difference is too large to be random, there's statistically significant evidence [*_against_*]{color=red} [the hypothesis]{color=red}.

@notes

Source: lect07d-chi_square.odp, slide 2.

=== layout: one-panel

# *Chi-Square (&chi;^2^) Terminology*

@body

- [Null Hypothesis (H~0~)]{color=red}\:
  - What we aim to find evidence against ([want to reject]{color=red}).
  - Always simpler to calculate the expected numbers.
- [Alternative Hypothesis (H~a~)]{color=green}\: Aim to support indirectly through the test.
- [Significance Level (&#945;)]{color=#A719DB}\: A preset threshold for statistical significance, usually set at 0.05.
- The [p-value]{color=#BE480A}\: The probability of getting results at least as extreme as those observed if the null hypothesis is true.

@notes

Source: lect07d-chi_square.odp, slide 3.
Instructor-approved four-slide split of original slides 3 and 4.

=== layout: one-panel

# *Chi-Square (&chi;^2^) Terminology*

@body

- [Chi-Squared statistic]{color=#861141}\: The calculated measure that quantifies the difference between observed and expected data.
- Critical Value\: A threshold for comparison with the [Chi-Squared statistic]{color=#861141}.
  - The [Critical Value]{color=blue} depends BOTH on the [Degrees of Freedom]{color=#B47804} and [Significance Level (&#945;)]{color=#A719DB}.
  - The [Critical Value]{color=blue} is obtained by looking at the *Chi-Square (&chi;^2^) Distribution Table*.
- [Degrees of Freedom]{color=#B47804}\: The number of independent values that can vary in an analysis.

@notes

Source: lect07d-chi_square.odp, slide 3.
Instructor-approved four-slide split of original slides 3 and 4.

=== layout: one-panel

# [Null hypothesis\:]{color=red} Reject or Do Not Reject?

@body

- If the [null hypothesis is rejected]{color=red}, then we [support the alternative hypothesis]{color=#00A933}.
  - Example\: [Null hypothesis\: &#8220;our situation is unbiased&#8221;]{color=red} [Alternative hypothesis &#8220;our situation is biased&#8221;]{color=#00A933}
- [Chi-squared statistic]{color=#650953} vs. [Critical value]{color=blue}\:
  - Larger [Chi-squared statistic]{color=#650953} values provide stronger evidence [against the null hypothesis]{color=red}.
    - [Do not reject null hypothesis]{color=green} if [Chi-squared statistic]{color=#650953} is less than or equal to [Critical value]{color=blue}.
    - [Reject null hypothesis]{color=red} if [Chi-squared statistic]{color=#650953} is greater than [Critical value]{color=blue}.
  - Example\: [Chi-squared statistic]{color=#650953} of 21.4 > [Critical value]{color=blue} of 7.82 &#8658; [Reject H~0~]{color=red}.
- [Critical value]{color=blue} depends BOTH on the [Degrees of Freedom]{color=#B47804} and [Significance Level (&#945;)]{color=#A719DB}.

@notes

Source: lect07d-chi_square.odp, slide 4.
Instructor-approved four-slide split of original slides 3 and 4.

=== layout: one-panel

# [Null hypothesis\:]{color=red} Reject or Do Not Reject?

@body

- [p-value]{color=#BE480A} vs. [significance level (&#945;)]{color=#A719DB}\:
  - A larger [Chi-squared statistic]{color=#650953} correlates with a smaller [p-value]{color=#BE480A} for fixed degrees of freedom.
    - [Reject null hypothesis]{color=red} if [p-value]{color=#BE480A} is less than [significance level (&#945;)]{color=#A719DB}.
    - [Do not reject null hypothesis]{color=green} if [p-value]{color=#BE480A} is greater than or equal to [significance level (&#945;)]{color=#A719DB} (fail to reject).
  - Example\: [p-value]{color=#BE480A} of 0.02 < fixed [significance level (&#945;)]{color=#A719DB} of 0.05 &#8658; [Reject H~0~]{color=red}.

@notes

Source: lect07d-chi_square.odp, slide 4.
Instructor-approved four-slide split of original slides 3 and 4.

=== layout: one-panel

# *Seven Steps of the Chi-Square (&chi;^2^) Test*

@body

- 1. [State]{color=#D40000} Hypothesis
  - _choose one that is easy to predict progeny_
- 2. [Predict]{color=#003FFF} expected progeny from hypothesis
- 3. [Subtract]{color=#007A00} expected from observed (o-e)
  - _square the value and then divide by expected_
- 4. [Sum]{color=#C80085} each row to get the Chi Square statistic (&chi;^2^) value
- 5. [Determine]{color=#935D00} degrees of freedom (DF)
- 6. [Look]{color=#00775F} up p-value using [#4]{color=#C80085} and [#5]{color=#935D00}
- 7. [Determine]{color=#A719DB} if hypothesis is rejected.

@notes

Source: lect07d-chi_square.odp, slide 5.

